  "notes": "Morocco has no strict endemics, and most of its birds are modelled as rare in the database, so the list is hand-picked: what a visitor meets on a standard tour (Marrakech and the High Atlas, Atlantic coast and Merja Zerga, Souss-Massa, Ouarzazate and the Draa valley, Boumalne Dades, Merzouga and the Middle Atlas), plus the Maghreb specialities and near-endemics (Atlas Flycatcher, Maghreb Magpie, Maghreb Owl, Levaillant's Woodpecker, Moussier's Redstart, Atlas Wheatear, African Blue Tit, Double-spurred Spurfowl, Northern Bald Ibis, Dupont's Lark, Desert Sparrow). Vagrants, scarce winter ducks, pelagic seabirds and escaped cage birds are dropped; Common Chaffinch and Eurasian Nuthatch are dropped because the Moroccan birds are African Chaffinch and the Atlas form is not split in the database. Regional specialities are not recorded separately: the Maghreb birds sit in their normal units. The main chapter is Lookalikes from home.",
